  "documentation": {
    "range": "The species is recorded from Pakistan, India, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Bangladesh, Myanmar, Malaysia, Indonesia, Laos, Cambodia, Viet Nam, Thailand and southern China. Possibly present in Bhutan. In Sri Lanka, <em>Channa striata</em> is commonly found in marshes, tanks, rivers and reservoirs throughout the low country both wet and dry zone except highlands. It is more common in the dry zone than the wet zone (De Silva <em>et al.</em> 2015).",
    "population": "The species is widespread and abundant across its range.",
    "habitats": "<em>Channa striata </em>inhabits swamps, freshwater ponds, streams and tanks in the plains; prefers stagnant muddy waters and grassy tanks. It breeds in the rainy season and, in Sri Lanka,<em> Channa striata</em> adults inhabit ponds, streams and rivers, preferring stagnant and muddy water of plains. This species can be found mainly in swamps, but also occurs in the lowland rivers. However, it is more common in relatively deep (1-2 m), still water and very common in freshwater plains. It also occurs in medium to large rivers, brooks, flooded fields and stagnant waters including sluggish flowing canals (De Silva <em>et al</em>. 2015, Pethiyagoda 1991, Senanayake 1980).",
    "threats": "Local populations in Sri Lanka suffer habitat loss and degradation, competition and predation from invasive alien species species, siltation, and water pollution. Overfishing is also a major concern across its range.",
    "measures": "There are no conservation actions in place for this species.",
    "use_trade": "This is an important food fish for local use and trade. This species is fairly common in the plains of India and is the mainstay of the tank fishery in Tamil Nadu. It breeds mostly during the rainy season; the female matures at a size of about 30 cm when two years old, and lays from a few hundred to over a thousand amber-coloured eggs. A popular and highly priced fish especially when freshly caught. This species is the most widely distributed and economically important member of the genus. It can reach a length of 60 to 75 cm; common size is 30 to 40 cm. It is cultivated in India, Myanmar and Pakistan; tolerates slightly brackish water.&#160;<em>C. striata</em> is known to be an excellent source of protein and is used for human consumption in Sri Lankan dry zone (De Silva <em>et al.</em> 2015, Pethiyagoda 1991).",
    "rationale": "As currently understood<span style=\"font-style: italic;\"></span>, <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">Channa striata</span>&#160;is a species complex. It is a widespread species with no major widespread threats and is currently assessed as Least Concern. However, the species requires taxonomic review and it should be re-assessed when this has been undertaken.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "<p>As presently understood, <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">Channa striata </span>is a species complex.<br/></p>"
  },
